-- run 1
Index Scan using lesson_progress_course_id_idx on lesson_progress  (cost=0.56..48210.33 rows=41500 width=32) (actual time=0.912..1838.207 rows=41022 loops=1)
  Index Cond: (course_id = 42)
  Buffers: shared hit=180 read=41022
Execution Time: 1843.118 ms

-- run 2
Index Scan using lesson_progress_course_id_idx on lesson_progress  (cost=0.56..48210.33 rows=41500 width=32) (actual time=0.021..95.640 rows=41022 loops=1)
  Index Cond: (course_id = 42)
  Buffers: shared hit=41202
Execution Time: 97.304 ms

Statement: "Run 2 did the same work as run 1 - the same plan touching the same 41,202 pages - and was faster only because the pages were already in shared_buffers."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The plan, the estimate and the row count are identical, and the page count is identical too: 180 + 41,022 = 41,202 pages in run 1, 41,202 in run 2. The only difference is where the pages came from: read means PostgreSQL asked the operating system for them (from disk or from the OS page cache), hit means they were already in shared_buffers. So the 19x difference is the cache, not the query. That is why comparing a cold first run before a change with a warm second run after it proves nothing: compare runs in the same cache state, and compare the buffer counts, which do not depend on the cache.', 1, 5
