SELECT last_autoanalyze, n_mod_since_analyze
FROM pg_stat_user_tables WHERE relname = ''lesson_progress'';

    last_autoanalyze    | n_mod_since_analyze
------------------------+---------------------
 2026-10-27 06:02:10+00 |                3120

Estimates for each filter on its own (EXPLAIN ANALYZE, first line):
  WHERE course_id = 7     ->  rows=240000 estimated, rows=239612 actual
  WHERE lesson_id = 701   ->  rows=10800 estimated,  rows=10812 actual

EXPLAIN ANALYZE SELECT * FROM lesson_progress WHERE course_id = 7 AND lesson_id = 701;

Index Scan using lesson_progress_lesson_id_idx on lesson_progress  (cost=0.43..10439.80 rows=216 width=32) (actual time=0.052..38.114 rows=10812 loops=1)
  Index Cond: (lesson_id = 701)
  Filter: (course_id = 7)

Statement: "The estimate of 216 rows is wrong because the statistics are stale; running ANALYZE lesson_progress will bring it close to 10,812."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The statistics are fresh (analyzed this morning, 3,120 changes since) and each filter on its own is estimated almost exactly. 216 is 12,000,000 x 0.02 x 0.0009: the planner multiplies the two selectivities because it assumes the columns are independent. Here they are not - every row of lesson 701 is also a row of course 7 - so the second filter removes nothing, and another ANALYZE produces the same 216. The fix is to tell the planner about the dependency with CREATE STATISTICS lp_course_lesson (dependencies) ON course_id, lesson_id FROM lesson_progress followed by ANALYZE, or to drop the redundant course_id filter. The misconception is that every misestimate means stale statistics; check pg_stat_user_tables and the single-column estimates before you run ANALYZE and hope.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_pgp_131') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
