ERROR:  deadlock detected
DETAIL:  Process 6012 waits for ShareLock on transaction 9120443; blocked by process 6087.
        Process 6087 waits for ShareLock on transaction 9120441; blocked by process 6012.
        Process 6012: UPDATE enrollments SET status = ''active'' WHERE id = 3002
        Process 6087: UPDATE enrollments SET status = ''cancelled'' WHERE id = 1001
HINT:  See server log for query details.
CONTEXT:  while updating tuple (1840,7) in relation "enrollments"

Earlier in the same transactions, process 6012 had updated id 1001 and process 6087 had updated id 3002.

Statement: "If both jobs lock the rows they will change in ascending id order before updating them, for example with SELECT id FROM enrollments WHERE id = ANY($1) ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE, these two jobs can no longer deadlock with each other on these rows."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. A deadlock needs a cycle: 6012 holds 1001 and wants 3002, while 6087 holds 3002 and wants 1001. When every transaction acquires its row locks in the same global order (here ascending id), the one that gets 1001 first also gets to 3002 first and the other simply waits, so no cycle can form. SELECT ... ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE takes the locks in that order up front. Waiting for ShareLock on a transaction is how PostgreSQL shows a wait for a row lock held by that transaction. Retrying on SQLSTATE 40P01 is still good practice, but it handles deadlocks; a consistent lock order removes this one. The misconception is that deadlocks are random bad luck that only retries can address.', 2, 5
