Statement: "enrollments_user_id_idx is a reasonable candidate to drop: it has not been scanned in 45 days, and the unique index on (user_id, course_id) can serve lookups by user_id alone."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. idx_scan = 0 with an empty last_idx_scan means no query used the index since the statistics reset, and a B-tree on (user_id, course_id) can answer WHERE user_id = $1 by its leading column, so the single-column index duplicates it. Meanwhile every INSERT into enrollments and every non-HOT UPDATE still writes an entry into its 412 MB. Before dropping it, check what statistics cannot show: usage on replicas (counters are per server; here there are none), a job that runs less often than the statistics window, and whether a constraint depends on it (it does not: it is not unique). Then drop it with DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY and keep its definition so it can be recreated. Note the opposite trap: a unique index with idx_scan = 0 can still be essential, because enforcing uniqueness is not counted as a scan. The misconception is that every index pays for itself just by existing.', 1, 5
