CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY users_last_activity_at_idx ON users (last_activity_at);

The hottest statement on users runs on every finished lesson:

UPDATE users SET xp = xp + 10, last_activity_at = now() WHERE id = $1;

pg_stat_user_tables for users, 7 days before and 7 days after the migration:

           | n_tup_upd  | n_tup_hot_upd | WAL per day (pg_stat_wal)
-----------+------------+---------------+--------------------------
 before    | 41,220,118 |    39,571,313 | 38 GB
 after     | 41,906,550 |     1,173,383 | 61 GB

Statement: "The drop in HOT updates is not caused by the new index: an index on last_activity_at only adds cost to queries that filter on last_activity_at."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. An UPDATE can be HOT (heap-only tuple) only when it changes no indexed column and the new row version fits on the same page. Before the migration the hot UPDATE changed xp and last_activity_at, neither indexed, so 96% of updates were HOT and touched no index. Now last_activity_at is indexed, so every one of those updates is non-HOT: it inserts a new entry into every index of users, not only the new one, which shows up as more WAL, more index bloat and more vacuum work. The cost of an index is paid by every write that touches an indexed column, whether or not anyone reads it. Options: drop it and let the weekly report scan the table, or index a column that is not updated on every request. The misconception is that an index only costs the queries that use it.', 2, 5
